Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "offensive lineup changes",
    "shot mix",
    "personnel effects",
    "shot type distribution",
    "five-man lineup units"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "detecting personnel effects via shot mix",
    "causal identification of lineup effects on shot selection",
    "study design sensitivity"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-009",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "With lineup FE the PULL_UP decline slope was -0.0255; without lineup FE it was -0.0063 and the same-shooter placebo on other play types was -0.0071 to -0.0075, consistent with shooter-level confound and the apparent effect being an artifact."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-012",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "The published suppression excess was reproduced but a within-unit permutation null reproduced it (0.3798 vs 0.3744, z 0.40), and drift-centering removed tail asymmetry, showing the excess is mechanical under the selection rule."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF09",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "Opponent POST_UP then PUTBACK stacking effect (-0.071) was smaller than either alone and far below the additive prediction (-0.232); per-player stacked n often under 5 and one cluster gradient had an unexpected sign."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-021",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "Penalty contrast (3→4 fouls) on any_fta was -0.151 but placebos were -5.556 and -9.655; effect not distinct from placebo, standard errors not printed, rendering the result inconclusive."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-009",
      "what_failed": "Lack of lineup fixed effects and shooter-level controls",
      "why": "Shooter selection confounded the exposure-effect estimate; the effect disappeared with lineup FE and was mirrored by a same-shooter placebo."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-012",
      "what_failed": "Extreme-event selection and matched-control comparison",
      "why": "The apparent excess was reproduced by a within-unit permutation null, indicating a mechanical artifact from the selection rule and drift."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF09",
      "what_failed": "Per-player stacked estimates",
      "why": "Small sample per player (n<5) and unexpected gradient signs made the stacking claim inconclusive and prone to implementation bugs."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-021",
      "what_failed": "Penalty-state effect tested against adjacent-foul placebos",
      "why": "The penalty contrast was not distinct from placebo contrasts and standard errors were unclear, failing to establish a genuine effect."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Without rigorous shooter-level controls, apparent lineup-related effects on play types can be entirely confounded by shooter selection.",
    "Mechanical floors and selection rules create spurious tail effects that vanish under permutation nulls and drift-centering.",
    "Small per-unit sample sizes and unvalidated placebos undermine claims of adaptation or additive stacking.",
    "Any test of shot mix changes after lineup substitutions must include same-shooter placebos and account for game-state confounders."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether a well-controlled within-game design can detect genuine shot mix differences caused by offensive five changes.",
    "The magnitude of any shot mix shift from typical substitutions or the power needed to detect it with available data.",
    "How to optimally aggregate play types or substitution windows to balance power and interpretability."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "A test of the design's ability to detect personnel effects requires within-game comparisons that isolate the effect of lineup changes from shooter identity, score, period, and defensive adjustments.",
    "The historical evidence directly motivates a design with shooter fixed effects, same-shooter placebo contrasts, and pre-registered aggregation to avoid artifacts.",
    "Demonstrating sensitivity at all is a prerequisite before any more nuanced lineup or matchup study relies on this methodology."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The number of clean offensive five changes with sufficient surrounding possessions may be too small to detect modest shifts in individual play types.",
    "Play type classification from play-by-play is noisy, and misclassification could attenuate or inflate apparent changes.",
    "The optimal window length around a substitution and the granularity of play type aggregation are not known a priori.",
    "Defensive lineup changes often co-occur with offensive substitutions, complicating causal attribution."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "Without the historical evidence, a naive comparison of shot mix before and after substitutions would likely produce spurious findings from shooter confounds, mechanical artifacts, and weak placebos. The retrieved records show that such confounds can create large apparent effects, which forces the design to incorporate shooter fixed effects, same-shooter placebo tests, and drift-aware inference. This changes the decision from 'proceed with a simple pre-post' to 'proceed with changes that directly address these failure modes'."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.65,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "The number of qualifying substitution events (offensive five change with stable stint context) may be limited, and power to detect a 5 percentage-point shift in moderately frequent play types is uncertain without pilot simulations."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Shooter selection, co-occurring defensive changes, and game situation are strong confounders that require within-game fixed effects and shooter-level controls."
      },
      {
        "code": "MECHANICAL_FLOOR",
        "text": "Shot mix proportions are bounded; floor/ceiling effects at the extremes can mask or exaggerate the change, especially for rare play types."
      },
      {
        "code": "BASE_RATE",
        "text": "Rare play types (e.g., post-ups, off-screen) have low base rates; detecting a meaningful shift would require unfeasibly many events."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [
      "FAM:SF-TMP-009",
      "FAM:SF-TMP-012",
      "FAM:SF09",
      "FAM:SF-TMP-021"
    ],
    "summary": "Earlier project families demonstrated that lineup-level effects on play-type outcomes are confounded by shooter selection (TMP-009), that mechanical artifacts and drift create spurious tail suppression (TMP-012), and that small per-player samples and weak placebos undermine adaptation claims (SF09, TMP-021). None directly tested whether a properly controlled design can detect shot mix shifts from offensive five changes, so the validation question remains open."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "Power may be inadequate because the number of substitutions with stable stint context across three seasons may not yield enough observations for simultaneous inference on multiple play types."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Many five-man lineups appear infrequently; aggregating over many substitutions without careful covariate adjustment risks confounding by game context."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Derived play-type labels from play-by-play are subject to classification error, which could dilute real effects or create false positives if systematic."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Shooter identity, defensive matchups, score margin, and clock all simultaneously change, making simple pre/post comparisons invalid."
    },
    {
      "code": "PLACEBO_DEPENDENCE",
      "text": "If the same-shooter placebo fails (i.e., shot mix changes for players whose roles shouldn't change), the entire design loses credibility."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Testing multiple play type families and substitution triggers without pre-specified multiplicity correction risks inflated false-positive rates."
    },
    {
      "code": "IMPLEMENTATION_BUG",
      "text": "Complex play-type linkage and stint windowing code is prone to off-by-one errors, incorrect merger of defensive matchups, and possession misalignment."
    },
    {
      "code": "DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY",
      "text": "Shot mix trends over the season or rule changes could produce spurious changes around substitutions if not modeled; drift-centering must be validated."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.35,
    "rejected": 0.15,
    "untestable": 0.20,
    "artifact": 0.15,
    "uncertain": 0.15
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The average change in the proportion of possessions ending in a specified play type (e.g., isolation, pick-and-roll ball-handler, post-up) immediately after a substitution that changes the offensive five, relative to the possession-weighted mean in the preceding possessions, net of shooter identity, game state, and defensive unit fixed effects. The design's sensitivity is declared adequate if a detectable shift exists for at least one common play type family when the same-shooter placebo is null.",
    "unit": "A possession, grouped into pre‑substitution and post‑substitution windows within the same continuous stint (no timeouts, quarter breaks, or other substitutions between). Each window contains a fixed number of possessions (e.g., 5) to balance stability and immediacy; the substitution itself is excluded.",
    "sample": "All NBA regular-season and playoff games from 2022‑23 through 2024‑25. Eligible substitutions: an offensive possession precedes the substitution; the substitution changes at least one offensive player; the offense remains in the same stint for at least 5 possessions on each side; the game is not in garbage time (score margin >20 in the 4th quarter, or >25 at any point, defined pre‑registry); overtime periods and heaves at the end of quarters are excluded.",
    "power": "Power will be assessed via simulation using the observed distribution of substitution events and empirical variance of shot-type proportions. The target is 80% power to detect a 5 percentage‑point shift in a play type with a baseline frequency of at least 15%, using a two‑sided test at α = 0.05 after Holm correction across 5 pre‑defined play-type families.",
    "controls": "Offensive team × season fixed effects; period fixed effects; score margin (linear); possession time remaining (polynomial up to quadratic); shooter fixed effects (the player initiating the play type). Defensive matchup fixed effects will be included for the primary on‑ball defender, obtained from the matchup data. Standard errors clustered at the team‑game level.",
    "falsification": "Primary placebo: same‑shooter placebo. For every substitution, identify offensive players who are on the court both before and after the change but whose offensive role is unaffected (e.g., spot‑up shooters not initiating the play). Test whether their shot mix changes. A significant result would invalidate the design. Secondary placebo: randomly permute the substitution boundary within the same stint and recompute the estimator; the observed estimate should fall within the central 95% of the permutation distribution.",
    "multiplicity": "Shot types are aggregated into 5 families (rim attacks, mid‑range, three‑point, post‑up, isolation) to reduce multiplicity. The primary test is a joint Wald test of no change across families; if rejected, we then test each family with Holm‑adjusted p‑values. Sensitivity is declared if at least one family survives correction and its same‑shooter placebo contrast is non‑significant.",
    "decision_rule": "If the joint test across play-type families is significant at α = 0.05, at least one family’s Holm‑adjusted p‑value < 0.05, and the family‑specific same‑shooter placebo p‑value > 0.10, the design is deemed sensitive to personnel effects. Otherwise the test is inconclusive or indicates insufficient sensitivity.",
    "data_version": "NBA stats API snapshot as of 2026-11-02 covering seasons 2022‑23, 2023‑24, 2024‑25; derived play‑type possession records from the graph database build (commit hash to be pre‑registered)."
  }
}

